{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_gyftmknmorlbm5xjzthg47pozn6yv4ksomo7jn75guxuxsz5mlga","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All tests pass. The implementation works correctly:\n\n- **Build**: Compiles cleanly (only warnings about non-upper-case constant names, which are part of the fixed interface and cannot be modified per the rules).\n- **Tests**: 1 passed, 0 failed.\n\nThe implementation translates the C Gorilla floating-point compression library to Rust:\n\n1. **`BitWriter`** — Bit-level writer using a 1024-byte cache with bit/byte/bits/flush operations.\n2. **`BitReader`** — Bit-level reader with a 64-bit buffer that loads bytes in big-endian order. Uses lifetime extension via `unsafe transmute` to reborrow into `self.b` (which is owned by the `FloatDecoder` containing the reader) — a necessary evil because the C struct holds a raw pointer into its own buffer field.\n3. **`FloatEncoder`** — XOR-delta encoding with leading/trailing-zero meta and significant bits, matching the C bit-packing exactly (including the `0x10` header byte and NaN-terminator on flush).\n4. **`FloatDecoder`** — Mirror of the encoder; `read_next_internal` follows the C `read_next` logic that actually operates on the decoder.\n5. **Helpers** — `bitslen`, `leading_zero64`, and `trailing_zero64` (using the de Bruijn sequence lookup) reproduce the C bit-counting routines.\n\nNote: the constant naming warnings (`de_bruijn64`, `de_bruijn64_tab`, `len8_tab`, `Nan`) come from the pre-existing interface which the task rules forbid modifying."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1071,"cache_read_input_tokens":39015,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1071,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73dbfa81-8f30-4a89-af20-0318ff8a751c","uuid